#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# Refuse feature work in the SHARED main checkout. `AGENTS.md` says worktree first; nothing enforced
# it, and the rule was broken three times in one session by the agent who had just written it up
# twice — once a tool fix and once a `rest_read.rs` change were swept into siblings' coordination
# commits and published. The failure is mechanical, so the fix is too.
#
# Modelled on scalascript's, which caught the same class of mistake on the first attempt.
#
# WHAT THIS MUST NOT BREAK, because the shared checkout is where they happen:
#   * merges — `git merge --no-ff feature/x` stages the whole branch here, every time;
#   * rebases, cherry-picks, reverts — same shape, machine-generated commits;
#   * a worktree's own commits — `core.hooksPath` applies to them too, so this hook runs there and
#     must stay out of the way.
# Each has a test in `scripts/githooks/test-pre-commit.sh`. If you change this file, run it.

set -euo pipefail

# A worktree's `.git` is a FILE pointing at the real dir; the shared checkout's is a DIRECTORY.
# This is the same test `AGENTS.md` and the multi-agent skill already use.
git_common="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)"
git_dir="$(git rev-parse --git-dir)"
if [ "$git_dir" != "$git_common" ]; then
  exit 0   # in a worktree — this hook has nothing to say
fi

# Machine-made commits: a merge, a rebase step, a cherry-pick, a conflicted revert. Their content is
# not a human deciding to edit a file here, and refusing them would break the one workflow the
# shared checkout exists for.
#
# KNOWN LIMIT, tested rather than hidden: a CLEAN `git revert` leaves no marker at all — git only
# writes REVERT_HEAD when the revert conflicts — and `pre-commit` receives no arguments and no
# GIT_REFLOG_ACTION to tell it apart from a hand edit. So a clean revert here is refused and needs
# `--no-verify`. Weakening the rule to admit it would admit every hand edit too, which is the whole
# thing this guards.
for marker in MERGE_HEAD CHERRY_PICK_HEAD REVERT_HEAD rebase-merge rebase-apply sequencer; do
  [ -e "$git_common/$marker" ] && exit 0
done

# Coordination is what this checkout IS for: claims and the boards.
allowed_re='^(\.work/|SPRINT\.md$|BACKLOG\.md$|BUGS\.md$|CHANGELOG\.md$|REPOS\.md$)'

staged="$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRT)"
[ -z "$staged" ] && exit 0

offending="$(printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -Ev "$allowed_re" || true)"
[ -z "$offending" ] && exit 0

name="$(printf '%s' "${offending%%$'\n'*}" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9' '-' | cut -c1-24)"
cat >&2 <<EOF

✋ pre-commit: this is the SHARED checkout — feature work belongs in a worktree.

Why: an edit left here gets swept into whichever sibling commits next, and published under their
name. That happened three times on 2026-08-06..08 before this hook existed.

Staged paths that are not coordination:
$(printf '%s\n' "$offending" | sed 's/^/  /')

Move it:
  git worktree add .worktrees/feature/<name> -b feature/<name> origin/master
  # then re-apply there; `git diff > /tmp/x.patch` before `git checkout --` if it is already edited

Coordination commits ARE allowed here: .work/**, SPRINT.md, BACKLOG.md, BUGS.md, CHANGELOG.md,
REPOS.md. So are merges, rebases and cherry-picks — they are what this checkout is for.

Doing a clean \`git revert\` on master? That one IS refused, and it is the hook's known limit: git
writes no marker for a revert that applies cleanly, so this cannot tell it from a hand edit. Use
--no-verify; you are not the case this is guarding against.

Override (rarely correct): git commit --no-verify
EOF
exit 1
